```

## Log Storage

Messages are stored in an append-only binary log.

Each record has the format:

```text
[4-byte message length][message bytes]
```

For example:

```text
┌──────────────┬─────────────────┐
│ length (4 B) │ message bytes   │
└──────────────┴─────────────────┘
```

The log maintains an in-memory index:

```text
logical offset → physical byte position
```

Example:

```python
{
    0: 0,
    1: 15,
    2: 33
}
```

The logical offset identifies the message, while the physical position tells the broker where that message starts in the file.

When the broker starts again, the index is rebuilt from the persistent log.

## Partitions

A `Partition` provides a higher-level abstraction over a log.

```text
Partition
    │
    └── Log
          │
          └── disk
```

Each partition has its own log and its own offset sequence.

Partitions are independent:

```text
orders
├── partition 0 → 0.log
├── partition 1 → 1.log
└── partition 2 → 2.log
```

## TCP Protocol

The current protocol uses newline-delimited requests.

Append:

```text
APPEND <message>
```

Read:

```text
READ <offset>
```

Responses are also newline-delimited:

```text
OK <offset>
OK <message>
EMPTY
ERROR <reason>
```

Because TCP is a byte stream rather than a message protocol, the broker maintains a receive buffer and extracts complete requests whenever `\n` is encountered.

## Consumer Polling

The consumer maintains the next offset it needs to read.

If the requested offset does not exist yet, the broker returns:

```text
EMPTY
```

The consumer then waits and polls again.

Conceptually:

```text
READ 32
   │
   ├── message exists → return message
   │
   └── no message
          │
          ▼
       wait
          │
          ▼
       READ 32
```

The next stage will eventually replace aggressive polling with more realistic polling/long-polling behavior.

## Testing

Tests use pytest.

Run all tests:

```powershell
pytest
```

Run partition tests:

```powershell
pytest tests/test_partition.py
```

The tests currently cover partition creation, reading, offset existence, and persistence.

